## 6. The `$batch` body

The fiddliest part of the build. Read all of it before writing any of it.

### 6.1 Creates and updates go in the SAME batch

You do **not** need separate create and update branches, and you do not need two `Send batch`
actions. A single batch carries both. The only differences between the two operations are the
URL, two headers, and nothing else — so one `Select` can emit both shapes and one HTTP action
sends them.

**Create** — no ID in the URL, no extra headers:

```
POST https://deutschebank.sharepoint.com/sites/simri/_api/web/lists(guid'6b659861-abd0-4e45-b74e-63e3f69f2648')/items HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json;odata=verbose
Accept: application/json;odata=verbose

{"__metadata":{"type":"SP.Data.Global_x0020_SIM_x0020_InventoryListItem"},"Title":"Popescu","ICC_ID":"8935201234567890125"}
```

**Update** — ID in the URL, plus `IF-MATCH` and `X-HTTP-Method`:

```
POST https://deutschebank.sharepoint.com/sites/simri/_api/web/lists(guid'6b659861-abd0-4e45-b74e-63e3f69f2648')/items(1042) HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json;odata=verbose
Accept: application/json;odata=verbose
IF-MATCH: *
X-HTTP-Method: MERGE

{"__metadata":{"type":"SP.Data.Global_x0020_SIM_x0020_InventoryListItem"},"ICC_ID":"8935201234567890125","Status":"Active"}
```

Notes on the update:

- **The method line stays `POST`.** `X-HTTP-Method: MERGE` is the override header; putting
  `MERGE` on the request line instead is the commonest reason updates 400.
- **`MERGE`, never `PUT`.** MERGE touches only the fields present in the body. PUT clears every
  field you omit — on this list that silently blanks two dozen columns.
- **`IF-MATCH: *`** means "overwrite regardless of version". If you'd rather a concurrent edit
  cause a failure instead of a silent overwrite, carry the item's `etag` from `Get inventory`
  and send that instead of `*`. With trigger concurrency set to 1, `*` is fine.
- **Never send `ID`** in the body of either shape. For an update it's the target, not a field;
  for a create SharePoint assigns it.

### 6.2 One changeset per operation

A changeset is an atomic unit in OData. Put all 100 writes in one changeset and a single bad
row can roll back the other 99 — which is exactly the behaviour you don't want, because your
report is built on per-row outcomes.

So wrap **each** operation in its own changeset. More verbose, but every row succeeds or fails
independently:

```
--batch_<runId>
Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary=changeset_<runId>_1

--changeset_<runId>_1
Content-Type: application/http
Content-Transfer-Encoding: binary

POST .../items(1042) HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json;odata=verbose
Accept: application/json;odata=verbose
IF-MATCH: *
X-HTTP-Method: MERGE

{"__metadata":{"type":"SP.Data.Global_x0020_SIM_x0020_InventoryListItem"},"Status":"Active"}

--changeset_<runId>_1--
--batch_<runId>
Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary=changeset_<runId>_2

--changeset_<runId>_2
Content-Type: application/http
Content-Transfer-Encoding: binary

POST .../items HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json;odata=verbose
Accept: application/json;odata=verbose

{"__metadata":{"type":"SP.Data.Global_x0020_SIM_x0020_InventoryListItem"},"FirstName":"Elena"}

--changeset_<runId>_2--
--batch_<runId>--
```

Blank lines are **significant** — one after each header block, one after the changeset
declaration. Line endings must be CRLF. The final boundary of each changeset and of the batch
itself gets a trailing `--`.

**Verify this rather than trust it.** Build a batch of 3 where the middle row is deliberately
invalid (e.g. a Status value the column rejects) and confirm rows 1 and 3 still commit. If
they don't, your changesets aren't isolating and the structure is wrong. Do this before
scaling up — it takes ten minutes and it's the difference between "12 rows failed" and "the
whole import silently rolled back".

### 6.3 Building the string in Power Automate

Don't loop. Use a `Select` to turn each decision into its sub-request string, then `join`.

**`Select batch parts`** — From: `outputs('Compose_batch_slice')`, single text-mode output:

```
--batch_@{variables('varRunId')}
Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary=changeset_@{variables('varRunId')}_@{item()?['excelRow']}

--changeset_@{variables('varRunId')}_@{item()?['excelRow']}
Content-Type: application/http
Content-Transfer-Encoding: binary

POST @{concat('https://deutschebank.sharepoint.com/sites/simri/_api/web/lists(guid''6b659861-abd0-4e45-b74e-63e3f69f2648'')/items', if(equals(item()?['action'],'update'), concat('(',item()?['spId'],')'), ''))} HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json;odata=verbose
Accept: application/json;odata=verbose
@{if(equals(item()?['action'],'update'), 'IF-MATCH: *
X-HTTP-Method: MERGE', '')}

@{concat('{"__metadata":{"type":"SP.Data.Global_x0020_SIM_x0020_InventoryListItem"},', substring(string(item()?['fields']), 1))}

--changeset_@{variables('varRunId')}_@{item()?['excelRow']}--
```

That last expression takes the `fields` object the script returned, drops its opening `{` with
`substring(..., 1)`, and prepends the `__metadata` block — giving a valid body without
enumerating 20 fields by hand.

Using `excelRow` as the changeset suffix is deliberate: when you parse the response, the
boundary tells you which spreadsheet row each result belongs to. Without that you're matching
responses to requests by position and praying nothing reordered.

**`Compose batch body`** — `@{concat(join(outputs('Select_batch_parts'), ''), '--batch_', variables('varRunId'), '--')}`

### 6.4 Two lookups you must do once

- **`__metadata.type`** — GET `_api/web/lists(guid'6b659861-abd0-4e45-b74e-63e3f69f2648')?$select=ListItemEntityTypeFullName`. Spaces become `_x0020_`, so "SIM Inventory" gives `SP.Data.Global_x0020_SIM_x0020_InventoryListItem`. Guessing this wrong 400s every operation.
- **Internal field names**, not display names — GET `_api/web/lists(guid'6b659861-abd0-4e45-b74e-63e3f69f2648')/fields?$select=Title,InternalName,TypeAsString&$filter=Hidden eq false and ReadOnlyField eq false`. `Legal entity` → `Legal_x0020_entity`, `SIM Type` → `SIM_x0020_Type`. Build the display→internal map once and keep it in a `Compose` at the top of the flow.

The script returns `fields` keyed by **Excel header name**. Either translate in the `Select`
that builds `fieldsJson`, or rename the columns in SharePoint so they match. Translating is
safer; renaming breaks any existing view or flow that references the old names.

### 6.5 ⚠️ Parsing the response — the trap

**`$batch` returns HTTP 200 even when every operation inside it failed.** Power Automate marks
the action green and moves on. A batch where all 100 writes were rejected looks identical to
one where all 100 succeeded.

The per-operation status lives in the multipart response body. After `Send batch`:

1. `Compose` the response body as a string
2. `split()` it on `--changesetresponse_` (or on the batch boundary in the response — inspect a real one, the names differ from what you sent)
3. Each part contains a line like `HTTP/1.1 204 No Content` for success or `HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request` followed by an `error` JSON object
4. `Filter array` for parts not containing `HTTP/1.1 2`
5. For each failure, append to `varBatchFailures` a report row shaped like the script's output — `excelRow` recovered from the changeset boundary, `action: "failed"`, and the SharePoint error message

That array is unioned into the report in §7, so batch-level failures surface next to validation
failures instead of vanishing.

If you skip this step, everything else in this document is decoration.

