## 8. `Send report` — Office 365 Outlook → Send an email (V2)

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| To | `triggerBody()?['text_2']` (ActionedBy) |
| Subject | `SIM import — @{triggerBody()?['text']} — @{variables('varFailed')} failed, @{variables('varCreated')} created, @{variables('varUpdated')} updated` |
| Attachment Name | `replace(variables('varFileName'),'.xlsx','.html')` |
| Attachment Content | `base64(outputs('Compose_report_html'))` |

Put the counts in the **subject line**. Most people will never open the attachment; the
subject is the report for them.

Body: a short summary plus the link to the saved copy. Keep the interactive version as the
attachment — email clients strip JavaScript from message bodies, so pasting the HTML inline
would give a dead, unfilterable table.

---

## 9. `Scope — Catch`

Configure run after → **has failed**, **is skipped**, **has timed out** on `Scope — Main`.

1. `Set varStatus` → `Failed`
2. `Send failure email` to ActionedBy **and** to you, with `result('Scope_-_Main')` in the body — that expression returns the status and error of every action inside, which is what you'll actually debug from
3. `Create item` in an `Import Log` list: runId, country, actionedBy, file name, status, error
4. `Terminate` with status `Failed`

Without the Terminate, a flow that failed halfway reports success.

---

## 10. Optional but worth it: `Import Log` list

One item per run — RunId, Country, ActionedBy, FileName, Started, Finished, Created, Updated,
Warning, Skipped, Failed, ReportUrl, Status. Write it at the end of both the main and catch
paths.

Flow run history expires (28 days on most plans). This list is what lets you answer "who
changed this SIM and when" a year from now.

